The problem is an instance of not-all-equal 3-SAT (equivalently, 2-coloring a signed 3-uniform hypergraph). An assignment in which \(x_i\) equals the parity of \(i\) already satisfies a large fraction of the clauses. The remaining monochromatic clauses (both the uniform-sign clauses whose three variables have identical parity and a subset of the mixed-sign clauses) form a collection of only a few dozen triples. A modest set of parity flips
\[
\begin{align*}
F_\text{odd}&=\{7,9,15,19,23,31,39,45,67,95,125,149,153,157,187,189\},\\
F_\text{even}&=\{6,14,18,44,64,94,104,116,130\}
\end{align*}
\]
hits every one of those triples in one or two positions. Each such hit toggles one or two literal values and therefore destroys monochromaticity, while the same flips leave every originally bichromatic clause still bichromatic. The resulting assignment therefore satisfies every gadget.

